Width [SimTalk] - FootPath
Sets the Width of the Footpath designated by <Path>.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.Width:length
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type length.
Note
In SimTalk 2.0 you can specify the length units m, mm, km, cm, yd, ft, and in.
Type in the unit directly after the values, without a separating blank space, for example 10m or 10.2m.
You can specify the unit for floating point values and for integer values.
Example
MyFootPath.Width := 2 // meters
See also
Width [text box] - FootPath
WorkerPool
WorkerPool
Use the object WorkerPool for modeling the lounge or the staff room of your plant.
Image
Width [SimTalk] - FootPath
Plant Simulation Help 11-3063
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Description
The Worker -WorkerPool-Workplace-FootPath-concept refines the Broker-Importer-Exporter-concept. The
WorkerPool represents the lounge or the staff room of your plant. The Workers are created in the
WorkerPool and they stay there when they do not work and are waiting for an order.
As a rule you type in the Names of the Workers, which you want to use in your simulation model, into
the Workers to Create table. You can also drag the Worker from the Class Library onto the icon of the
WorkerPool and drop him there. The WorkerPool appends a line to the Workers to Create table for this
Worker. When it already contains a line for this Worker, the WorkerPool increments the amount by 1.
You can also create individual Workers, which you do not want to generate via the Workers to Create
table, with the method create for a single simulation run. This only works in the WorkerPool though.
Be aware that Plant Simulation deletes this Worker when you reset the model and then only creates the
Workers from the Workers to Create table at the next Init event that takes place.
You can select the Travel Mode with which the Worker gets to his work station:
The Broker brokers the Worker to the individual work stations.
Note
If no Broker exists when creating the Workers, Plant Simulation sets the attribute AutomaticMediation
of the Workers to false.
As soon as a Worker can provide a service, the Broker sends him from his WorkerPool to the work station
that ordered him:
• When the Worker walks freely within the area of the simulation model, he walks on the shortest route to
the WorkPlace and walks around obstacles you designated.
The Worker consumes time, when he walks freely within the area to the Workplace.
• When you modeled the work station using one of the material flow objects and the Workplace and
connected the WorkerPool and the Workplace with a FootPath, Plant Simulation animates the Worker
while he moves on the FootPath and while he works on the Workplace.
The Worker consumes time, when he walks on the FootPath.
• If you activated Beam to workplace and you inserted a Workplace, and the Worker, who is supposed
to work there, cannot reach it via a FootPath, Plant Simulation transports the Worker directly to the
Workplace and animates him there.
• If you activated Workers can work remotely and if you did not insert a Workplace, if no Workplace
exists that supports this service, or if all Workplaces that support this service are occupied, Plant
WorkerPool
11-3064 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Simulation seemingly beams the Worker to the work station itself, where he will perform his task. It
does not animate him there, but continues to animate him in the WorkerPool.
If your plant works in shifts, the Worker walks back to his WorkerPool at the end of his shift. He then
deposits the parts he could not deliver into the Parts Buffer, which you assigned to the WorkerPool. The
parts buffer can be any one of the material flow objects, which accepts parts. When you did not assign a
parts buffer to the WorkerPool, Plant Simulation shows a warning and stops the simulation.
If you activated the transport importer in the parts buffer, one of the available Workers carries the parts
in the parts buffer to the destination object, after the next shift has started. If you did not activate the
transport importer, the parts in the parts buffer are moved on according to the Exit Strategy which you
selected for the parts buffer.
The WorkerPool looks like this:
The WorkerPool initially does not show any Worker at all. To see the Workers, select the check box Show
Content on the tab Graphics of the WorkerPool
