Workers to Create
To open the table, into which you type information pertaining to the Workers to be created and inserted
into your model, click this button.
Remarks
Note
Plant Simulation always resolves relative paths in relation to the Frame in which the WorkerPool is
inserted. If Plant Simulation cannot resolve a path in the Scope array during the simulation, it shows an
error message and asks if you would like to stop the simulation.
This also applies to the Home Location.
Click the 
  button to deactivate Inheritance 
 . Only then you can enter data into the table.
Proceed as follows:
• Type the class of the Worker, which the WorkerPool uses as a template for the Workers to be
created, into the cells below Worker. You might, for example, type in .Resources.Worker
or .UserObjects.MyWorker.
• Type the number of the Workers to be created into the cells below Amount.
• Type in the name of a Shift which you defined in the associated Shift Calendar [WorkerPool].
• If you want the Worker to walk with a different Speed than the one defined in its class, type this speed
into the cell below Speed. This deactivates inheritance for the speed, i.e., speed changes in the class
will not affect the Workers defined in this row. If you do not type in anything, the Worker walks with the
speed specified in the class, meaning he inherits that value from the class.
• Type in the Efficiency of the Workers to be created into the cells below Efficiency. You might, for
example, enter 80 for an efficiency of 80 percent.
• Type in the Home Location, i.e., the path to the Workplace to which the Worker walks, once he has
finished his current job.
Workers to Create
11-3070 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Type in the Scope, i.e., the path to the material flow objects to which the Worker can be brokered. The
scope also encompasses sub-Frames.
Proceed as follows:
◦ Click in the cell Scope of the Worker whose scope you want to set.
◦ This opens a table with one empty cell.
◦ Select the object in the Frame to which the Worker can be brokered and drop it into the cell of the
table.
Press Enter to add another cell and drag the next object to this new cell.
Repeat this as often as necessary.
◦ Click OK in the table. Plant Simulation then inserts the objects you added to the table into the cell
Scope of the Workers to Create table and separates them with a semicolon.
◦ Click OK in the window of the Workers to Create table.
• Type the services which the Worker can provide, in addition to those you entered into the Services list,
into the cells below Additional Services.
Workers to Create
Plant Simulation Help 11-3071
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

You can type different types of additional services into the columns to the right of Additional Services.
You can also leave gaps between the services in the table. This way you can type in identical services
one below the other, even if you want some Workers to not provide these services.
Note
The names of the services are not case-sensitive. The WorkerPool attempts to export the services in
the order in which you entered them into the table.
Note
To save memory and improve access speed, all places which use such a case-insensitive string point to
the same string in main memory. The visible and unexpected result is that the first occurrence of the
string defines how the string is written in terms of upper- and lower-casing.
In SimTalk you can compare if strings are case-insensitive with the ~= operator, compare Relational
Operators. The change in the Workers to Create table only affects the simulation run if the change
takes place before the initialization.
Plant Simulation enters the services you defined here into the list of offered services of the Broker: To view
them, select View > Offered Services.
You can also change the Workers to Create table within the Init Control of the WorkerPool for the
upcoming simulation run. This is not possible in an init method, as the method is executed too late in
time.
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Workers to Create
